# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your Supabase project's values
# (Project Settings → Data API). Only the anon PUBLIC key goes here — never
# the service_role key.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PROJECT IS, SINCE THE PUBLIC RELEASE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meridian now talks to TWO Supabase projects.
#
#   * The one named below is the AUTH project — yours. It holds sign-ups, sign-ins
#     and sessions for everybody, plus four small platform tables the push server
#     has to read across all users. Auth rows are tiny, so the free tier is not at
#     risk from other people using the app, and your dashboard shows a real count
#     of who signed up.
#
#   * Everyone else's actual DATA lives in a Supabase project they create and own.
#     They paste its URL and anon key into Meridian's setup walkthrough, those two
#     values are stored in their browser and nowhere else, and the app reads and
#     writes every module table there. Nothing about that reaches this project.
#
# Your own account is the exception, and VITE_OWNER_EMAIL below is what makes it
# one: it keeps using this project for data too, exactly as it always has.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-public-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner bypass — REQUIRED, or you will be sent through the setup flow yourself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The email address you sign in to Meridian with. An account matching it skips
# the bring-your-own-Supabase setup entirely and keeps reading and writing the
# project above, so nothing about your own day-to-day use changes.
#
# Not a secret: the match is against an address Supabase Auth issued, and
# addresses are unique per project, so nobody can claim yours by typing it.
# Comma-separate if you have more than one account.
#
# This has to be set in TWO places — here for local development, and in your
# Vercel project's environment variables for the deployed site. Miss the Vercel
# one and the live app will ask YOU to paste in Supabase credentials.
VITE_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional alternative to the above, keyed by Supabase user id instead of email
# (Authentication → Users → click yourself → User UID). Either is enough.
VITE_OWNER_USER_ID=

# Chronicle voice transcription (Groq Whisper). Deliberately NOT prefixed with
# VITE_ — Vite only exposes VITE_* to the browser bundle, so an unprefixed name
# cannot be leaked into client code by accident. This key is read by the Supabase
# Edge Function in supabase/functions/transcribe-voice, which is the only thing
# that ever talks to Groq.
#
# Deployed Edge Functions do NOT read this file. For anything other than a local
# `supabase functions serve`, set it as a project secret instead:
#   supabase secrets set GROQ_API_KEY=your_key_here
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web Push (notifications)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate all three at once — the script writes them straight into .env.local
# rather than printing the private key:
#
#   npm run vapid
#
# VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY is the ONLY one of these that belongs in the browser.
# Web Push works by shipping the server's public key to the client, so this being
# in the bundle is the design, not a leak. It also has to be set in your Vercel
# project's environment variables, or the deployed app cannot subscribe at all.
VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The private half. Server-side only, forever. Same rule as GROQ_API_KEY: no
# VITE_ prefix, and deployed Edge Functions do not read this file, so it must
# also be set as a Supabase project secret:
#   supabase secrets set VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=... VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# A contact address for the push services (RFC 8292 requires one, and some of
# them check it). Your real email, prefixed with mailto:.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# A random string of your own choosing. The push-dispatch function is deployed
# with --no-verify-jwt so pg_cron can call it, and this header is what stops
# anyone else who finds the URL from making it send. It goes in TWO places:
#   supabase secrets set CRON_SECRET=your_random_string
#   ...and into supabase/notifications_cron.sql before you run it.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE REAL VALUE. An earlier revision of notifications_cron.sql
# carried one, so that string is in this repository's git history permanently and
# must be treated as burned — see "Rotating the cron secret" in SETUP.md.
# Listed here only as a record of what you chose; nothing local reads it.
CRON_SECRET=
